### name: stmt.take.count-sel-refuses-is-a-refusal
--- note
Review 2026-09-15 finding U. A TAKE count SEL itself rejects -- `1/0` -- is
E_SQL_INVALID, the refusal every constant SEL rejects gets. Three hosts
threw their own TypeError out of translate_statement and plan_hybrid
instead, because the helper that turns a SEL error into that refusal was
private to the constants module.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items"}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> TAKE(1 / 0)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:17
===
### name: stmt.drop.count-sel-refuses-is-a-refusal
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items"}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> DROP(1 / 0)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:17
===

### name: stmt.filter.constant-true-is-a-where
--- note
A FILTER whose predicate is a constant TRUE over the bare relation is still a
FILTER: SEL keeps it because the source might be a scalar (conformance
agg.filter.scalar-source-with-constant-predicate), and the translator renders
what it is handed. One host's translator dropped it and rendered no WHERE.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "fields": {"QTY": {"kind": "column", "column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> FILTER(TRUE)
--- expect
SELECT * FROM `items` WHERE TRUE
===

### name: stmt.order-by.direction-that-is-not-a-literal-is-refused
--- note
The three-argument SORT_BY resolves like the evaluator's (spec §7.3): a text
literal third is the direction, otherwise a bare name second is the binder.
Neither here, so the third argument is a direction SQL cannot read -- the
same refusal, at the same slot, as the four-argument form gives. Three hosts
raised a host TypeError here; the fourth refused at the key.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "fields": {"QTY": {"kind": "column", "column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> SORT_BY(_["qty"], IF(TRUE, "DESC", "ASC"))
--- error
E_BAD_ARG 1:28
===

### name: stmt.order-by.four-argument-direction-that-is-not-a-literal-is-refused
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "fields": {"QTY": {"kind": "column", "column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> SORT_BY(r, r["qty"], IF(TRUE, "DESC", "ASC"))
--- error
E_BAD_ARG 1:31
===

### name: stmt.order-by.binder-form-with-a-computed-key
--- note
The binder form with a key that is not a field: `O` is the binder and the IF
is its key, a constant, so every row sorts equal. The direction is not the
IF's value, and no optimiser may fold the IF into the direction slot.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "fields": {"QTY": {"kind": "column", "column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> FILTER(_["qty"] > 0) .> SORT_BY(O, IF(TRUE, "DESC", "ASC"))
--- expect
SELECT * FROM `items` WHERE (`qty` > 0) ORDER BY CAST(CASE WHEN TRUE THEN 'DESC' ELSE 'ASC' END AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin ASC
===

### name: stmt.order-by.direction-is-case-insensitive
--- note
As in memory, where the direction is ASCII-upcased before it is checked.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "fields": {"QTY": {"kind": "column", "column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> SORT_BY(_["qty"], "desc")
--- expect
SELECT * FROM `items` ORDER BY `qty` DESC
===

### name: stmt.map.computed-field-then-keyless-sort
--- note
A keyless SORT after a MAP compares the MAP's outputs, so it must sort the
projection, not the source: the sign is flipped, and sorting `qty` first
would put the rows in the opposite order (conformance
rel.map.negation-then-keyless-sort). Two hosts' translators ran the
optimiser's MAP/SORT swap and rendered the source sorted.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "items", "fields": {"QTY": {"kind": "column", "column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ITEMS .> MAP(RECORD("q", 0 - _["qty"])) .> SORT()
--- expect
SELECT `_sub1`.* FROM (SELECT (0 - `qty`) AS `q` FROM `items`) `_sub1` ORDER BY `_sub1`.`q` ASC
===
